<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daylamites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dailamites, Deylamites, Daylamis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Daylamites were an Iranian people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>, the highlands south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, famed as tough infantry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. They resisted Arab rule for centuries and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the 9th and 10th centuries, often in its Zaydi form, through Alid missionaries. In the 10th century Daylamite dynasties, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Daylamites lived in the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a> range between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>. They spoke Daylami, a now-extinct Northwestern Iranian language similar to that of their neighbours the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilaks">Gilites</a>. They first appear in records in the late 2nd century BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> mentions them, though he miscalls them &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a>&quot;. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> they served as high-quality infantry. The Byzantine historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> described them as people who lived on inaccessible mountainsides, had never become subject to the Persian king and marched with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, each man carrying a sword, a shield and three javelins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Daylamites resisted the Arab conquest of their homeland for several centuries under their own local rulers, and warfare in the region was endemic, with raids and counter-raids on both sides. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> occupied parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a> but never controlled it effectively; al-Mansur proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against it in 759 or 760, and the campaigns brought little but spoils and captives. After 781 the Nestorian monk Shubhalishoʿ began converting them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> but made little headway. During the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alids</a> fleeing persecution began the gradual conversion of the Daylamites to Shia Islam, and in the 9th century many adopted Zaidi Islam.</p><p>From the 9th century Daylamite foot-soldiers became an important part of armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> and the Ghaznavids employed them, and they served in the Seljuq army. Between 934 and 936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla">Ali ibn Buya</a>, known as Imad al-Dawla, led the Daylamite conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>, and his brother Ahmad conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>. In 946 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> died in 983 he controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and parts of the Jazira, and held territories directly or indirectly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> to the Byzantine frontier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> to the Caspian. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> ended Buyid power in the mid-11th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Daylamites were most likely adherents of some form of Iranian paganism, with a minority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Nestorian Christians</a>. They fought on foot behind tall shields painted grey, forming a wall and using javelins and battle-axes from behind it; their short two-pronged spears could be thrust or thrown. Their lack of cavalry led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> to add Turkic horsemen to their armies. According to the geographer Estakhri they practised agriculture and kept herds but only a few horses, grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, fished and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> textiles. Daylamite women took part in agriculture like men, and the people strictly practised endogamy. The Zaza, who call themselves Dimli, are considered one of the Daylamite groups that spread westward during the expansion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daylamites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Buyid armies combined two very different forces: Daylamite infantry, the dynasty's own countrymen, fighting with spears, javelins and large shields, and Turkish cavalry, mostly slave soldiers or freedmen, who provided the mobility the <b>Daylamites</b> lacked.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Alongside them served Indian troops, including Hindu regiments with their own commanders, as well as Arabs, Kurds, <b>Daylamis</b> and Afghans, and a corps of war elephants taken or bought in India, used in Khurasan as well as on Indian campaigns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Hasan al-Utrush, known as al-Nasir al-Kabir, restored the state in 914 and converted many <b>Daylamites</b> and Gilites to Zaydi Islam, writing works of law still respected by Zaydis.</blockquote><a href="/state/alavid-dynasty">Alavid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Daylamites</b> were tough highland infantry who long resisted Arab rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/justanid-dynasty">Justanid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
